This is the lab sequence — work the steps in order; each one builds on the last.
1. Choose the dataset: real tables and real questions from real users — write down 20 questions before building anything.
2. Decide the agent type: text-to-SQL or quality agent — pick the one whose failure mode you can bound, not the flashier one.
3. Implement read-only guardrails first: role, timeouts, approval gates for anything beyond reads — before the agent answers its first question.
4. Build the question set: 20 questions with known-correct answers, including 5 unanswerable ones the agent must decline.
5. Evaluate: accuracy on answerables, decline rate on unanswerables, and the cost and guardrail log for every query.
6. Document limits: the questions it can't handle, the schema corners it misunderstands, the failure you saw twice — as a first-class deliverable.
7. Write it up: numbers, guardrails demonstrated, failures — the honest report that closes the data track.

