Before you trust the narrative, work the numbers. The example below is illustrative — every assumption is labeled so you can substitute your own.

Do the roadmap math for one agentic feature, honestly. Suppose v1 targets 3 workflows, each needing about 2 weeks of build (your team's velocity, not a vendor's), a 100-question golden eval set per workflow (8–17 hours of human verification each at 5–10 minutes per question — price it), and a 4-week pilot. That's roughly 6 build-weeks plus about 50 eval-hours plus pilot time before you know if any of it works — write your own numbers in. Build-vs-buy: list what you'd buy (orchestration, eval tooling, hosting) versus build (the workflow logic, the evals, the guardrails) and price both over 12 months, including the maintenance nobody puts in the demo. Cost modeling: per-task model cost times projected volume, plus the eval-maintenance cost as the product changes (evals rot — budget to refresh them quarterly). Sequencing: order the 3 workflows by low-risk times high-frequency times measurability, and show why the ambitious one goes last. The output is a one-page roadmap with costs, the sequencing rationale, and the explicit v1 non-goals — what v1 will not do, in writing, agreed with stakeholders.

