This is the lab sequence — work the steps in order; each one builds on the last.
1. List the management tasks you'd delegate: onboarding questions, review load, 1:1 prep, knowledge lookup — and mark which ones touch people data.
2. Build the onboarding assistant: grounded in your real docs (README, runbooks, architecture notes); test it with 10 questions a new hire actually asked, and check every answer.
3. Design review-load balancing: the agent suggests reviewers by expertise and current load — a human still makes the assignment.
4. Draft the 1:1 prep: the agent summarizes public work signals (merged PRs, docs written) — never private messages, never sentiment analysis of people.
5. Draw the privacy boundary: what people data the agent may never touch — written as a policy with concrete examples, not vibes.
6. Measure team-health signals honestly: pick 2 signals (for example review turnaround, doc freshness), define them precisely, and note what they cannot see.
7. Write the policy: allowed assists, the people-data boundary, and the review cadence for the policy itself — because policies rot too.

